<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=oil-well">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oil well</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">347 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An oil well is a hole bored into the Earth to bring petroleum to the surface; the earliest known were drilled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 347 CE. They were drilled with bits attached to bamboo poles to depths of up to about 240 metres, and their oil was burned to evaporate brine and produce salt. The first modern oil <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> were drilled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absheron-peninsula">Absheron Peninsula</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a> in 1846–1848, and in the 20th century rotary drilling largely replaced cable tools, reaching much greater depths in less time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oil-well.jpg" alt="Oil well" width="320" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Earlyoilfield.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>347 CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>China</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The first modern oil wells were drilled near Baku in 1846–1848.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/kerosene">Oil refining<span>9th century</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=oil-well">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
